  "contract": "Listing, updating and backfilling schedules in a fresh namespace. Listing covers the status and workflow type filters, the visibility query (equality predicates joined by AND on schedule fields and registered search attributes, which no schedule holds) and its refusals, unsupported filters and Laravel's messages, and signed continuation tokens with filter-mismatch, malformed and stale-cursor refusals. Updates cover merged actions, specifications in another zone, memo, quotas and PHP's changed fields. Backfills cover validation, an empty or reversed range, every cron and interval occurrence in a range across a daylight-saving change with PHP's fire times, repeated occurrences, skip, terminate_other and buffering policies, a two-run quota and a paused schedule. Every response matches published PHP's apart from generated identities, wall-clock times, fire lags and continuation token bytes. Published PHP on SQLite can lose a backfilled occurrence to database is locked against its own maintenance roles and records it as a failure; the probe then replays the whole sequence in a new namespace, which native must never need. Native accepts the ISO-8601 time forms only, and answers an update to an unevaluable specification with 422 where PHP returns 500; neither case is in this fixture. Embedded applications have no HTTP schedule surface."
